        "detail": "wall_window takes the window as the middle 60 per cent of the z range of the body's points inside the band. At C2 the body (anatomy.body_in_front) holds the dens, so that range runs from the lower plate to the tip of the dens and the window sits mostly on the dens. measure.SKIP leaves out only C1 and the sacrum, and no_dens_plate drops only the upper plate's keys. So C2's endplate_lower_incl, its pedicle_angle_sagittal and its spinous_angle are all taken against this wall, at every study whose field holds C2. Phantom: a cervical-sized C2 (W 17, D 16, H 14, canal 24) with a dens 9 mm across and 14 mm tall, read on 0.8x0.8x1 mm, grid 2 degrees off. Without the dens the wall is -0.12 off. With the dens's back flush and upright it is +2.60 off (freedom 0.84). With the back 1.5 mm proud and upright, +8.08 (freedom 0.62). Proud 1.5 mm and leaning back 12 degrees, +3.83 (freedom 0.34). On 2.5 mm slices the same cases read +2.31, +7.77 and +3.64, and proud 3.0 mm leaning back 12 reads +8.97 (freedom 1.96). None is refused or flagged. The edges' span goes from 8.1 mm to 16.1 mm once the dens is there. endplate_lower_incl moves with the wall: -3.37 without the dens becomes -7.27 for the dens proud 1.5 mm leaning 12. The reading this replaces, on the same 1.5/12 dens, was +2.31 on grid x2 and +0.72 on suite18; the new one is +3.83 and +3.12, so at C2 the change makes the reading worse. Logs: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/review_robustness/dens.log, dens_more_thin.log, dens_more_thick.log (scripts probe_label_edits.py, probe_dens_more.py in the same folder).",
        "fix": "Bound the window to where the wall really is the canal's front: at least for C2, end the window at the top of the canal's full rings (or at the dens's base), for example by intersecting [lo, hi] with the rings' z span. Otherwise refuse C2's wall, so its three angles are NAN with a flag. Add a C2-with-dens phantom test.",
        "lens": "robustness",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds in substance. I read the code and measured it on my own phantoms, with a dens drawn independently of the reviewer's: an elliptic cylinder 9 mm across and 10 mm deep, standing on the body's top.\n\nThe code path:\n- wall_window (body.py line 435 on) sets the window to the middle WALL_HEIGHT_SHARE (60 per cent) of the z range of body points within the band.\n- At C2 the body cut (anatomy.body_in_front, everything in front of the canal line) holds the dens. So the window runs up onto the dens, and the gated edges are taken there too.\n- measure.SKIP leaves out only C1 and the sacrum. no_dens_plate drops only the upper-plate keys in DENS.\n- So C2 keeps endplate_lower_incl, spinous_angle and pedicle_angle_sagittal. All three are taken against this wall: inclinations, posterior.spinous_angle, and pedicle.wall_reading, which goes through BODY.posterior_wall.\n- Neither the refusal nor the flag added by the change fires.\n\nWhat I measured. Scripts and logs are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_1_0/ (probe_c2.py, probe_c2_rows.py, c2_thin_H14_x2.log, c2_thick_H14.log, c2_thin_H22.log, c2_rows_thin_H14.log).\n- Cervical C2 at the reviewer's size (body 14 mm), 1 mm slices, grid tilted 2 degrees. Without a dens the wall is off by -0.12. With a dens it is off by +2.62 (back flush, upright), -2.49 (flush, leaning back 12), +5.98 (1 mm in front, upright), +1.82 (1 mm in front, leaning 12) and -6.94 (1 mm behind, leaning 12).\n- The same body on 2.5 mm slices: up to -7.10 on grid x2 and up to -9.16 on suite18.\n- In all of these the edges' span roughly doubles (8 to 16 mm), the freedom stays at 0.17 to 2.10 degrees, and no posterior_wall or posterior_wall_free flag is set. The only flag is posterior_wall_free, set on the dens-free baseline itself on thick slices.\n- The kept rows move one for one with the wall (thin, x2). endplate_lower_incl's error is -3.37 without the dens, -9.37 with the dens 1 mm in front and upright, and +3.54 with it 1 mm behind and leaning 12. spinous_angle's error goes from +0.54 to +6.68 and -6.23. Again no wall flag.\n- On this cervical phantom the pedicles' sagittal angle is NAN for reasons of its own, with or without a dens. So I could not show it carrying the error, though the code takes it against the same wall.\n\nWhere the claim overstates:\n- \"Mostly on the dens\" depends on how tall the body is next to the dens. The dens holds 46 to 49 per cent of the window with the reviewer's 14 mm body under a 14 mm dens, and 28 to 31 per cent with a taller 22 mm body.\n- With the taller body the errors shrink to 0.2 to 3.5 degrees, still unflagged: +3.37, +3.51 and +3.04 with the dens's back 1 mm in front of the wall, and 0.9 to 1.6 flush and upright.\n- \"The change makes C2 worse\" does not hold across shapes. The old reader used the same 60 per cent window of B's z range and errs at C2 too. Its errors were sometimes smaller (thick suite18, dens 1 mm behind, leaning 12: old -1.69, new -9.16) and sometimes larger (thick x2, flush, leaning 12: old -5.70, new -1.75; no dens, thin x2: old -4.63, new -0.12).\n- So the window's reach onto the dens was there before this change. The rewritten posterior_wall and its new flags carry it on, and catch none of it.\n\nThe finding stands as a real, unflagged defect at C2 in the changed code. Its reach depends on the body-to-dens proportion and the dens's offset, not mostly on the dens in every case.",
            "measured": "Phantom C2 (W 17, D 16, canal 24, with C3 and C4), grid not turned, dens an elliptic cylinder (half-widths 4.5 across and 5 front to back, 14 mm tall). Wall error in degrees, new reader (old reader in brackets):\n- Body 14 mm, 1 mm slices, grid x2. No dens -0.12 (-4.63). Dens back flush, upright +2.62 (+1.78). Flush, leaning 12: -2.49 (-1.90). 1 mm in front, upright: +5.98 (+5.92). 1 mm in front, leaning 12: +1.82 (+0.26). 1 mm behind, leaning 12: -6.94 (-2.05).\n- Body 14 mm, 2.5 mm slices. Grid x2: +2.49, -1.75, +6.37, +2.22, -7.10. Suite18: +1.33, -4.11, +6.05, +0.92, -9.16.\n- Body 22 mm, 1 mm slices, grids x0, x2 and suite18: from -3.17 to +3.51.\n- No posterior_wall or posterior_wall_free flag in any dens case. The edges' span goes from 8 to 16 mm at 14 mm and from 11-13 to 20-21 mm at 22 mm. The dens holds 0.46 to 0.49 of the window at 14 mm and 0.28 to 0.31 at 22 mm.\n- C2 rows from measure.vertebra_values (thin, x2). endplate_lower_incl error: -3.37 (no dens), -9.37 (1 mm in front, upright), +3.54 (1 mm behind, leaning 12). spinous_angle error: +0.54, +6.68, -6.23. The pedicles' sagittal angle is NAN for reasons of its own on this phantom."
          },
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds. I rebuilt the phantoms and got the same numbers. Some of its wording overstates the defect.\n\nWhat the code does. measure.SKIP is (\"C1\", \"sacrum\"), so C2 is built and measured. Its body is anatomy.body_in_front, everything connected in front of the canal line, so the dens is part of B. measure.no_dens_plate sets the DENS keys to NaN, but that list has no endplate_lower_incl, no pedicle_angle_sagittal* and no spinous_angle. All three are still read against body.posterior_wall. wall_window takes the middle 60 per cent of the z range of B's midline points, which at C2 runs from the lower plate to the tip of the dens. That window rule is the old reader's, carried over unchanged from a9f21fd. So the defect was inherited, not introduced, but the new reader keeps it, and its flags do not cover it.\n\nWhat I measured. My own probe (verify_1_1/dens_probe.py) used the reviewer's cervical sizes, a dens 9 mm across and 14 mm tall, and C2 built as measure_volume builds it. On the x2 grid, 1 mm slices:\n- no dens: -0.12\n- dens flush and upright: +2.60\n- base 1.5 mm in front of the wall, upright: +8.08\n- the same, leaning back 12 degrees: +3.83\n- On 2.5 mm slices the same three dens cases read +2.31, +7.77 and +3.64.\nThese are identical to the reviewer's logs. The edges' span goes from 8.1 to 16.1 mm. Freedom is 0.3 to 0.8 degrees, so no dens case is refused or flagged in measure_volume's C2 rows. spinous_angle follows the wall: +0.54 with no dens, then +3.31, +8.79 and +4.48.\n\nCorrections to the claim:\n1. \"Mostly on the dens\" goes too far. 35 to 50 per cent of the window and 22 to 41 per cent of the kept edges lie above the body's top.\n2. \"Proud\" is the wrong word. The reviewer's back +1.5 places the dens's base 1.5 mm in front of the wall, recessed. Leaning back 12 degrees, it is about 1.5 mm proud only at the tip.\n3. \"The change makes the reading worse\" is true only in some cases. On the x2 grid it is worse in every dens case. On suite18 with the grid turned 7 degrees it is mixed: flush 1.61 new against 1.75 old, recessed and leaning back 12 degrees 2.48 against 3.24, flush leaning back 10 degrees -2.24 against +1.51.\n4. On a taller body, closer to C2's own (20 mm, dens 15 mm), a dens flush and leaning back 10 degrees reads -0.79, -0.38 and -0.90, so the error is under a degree. A base recessed 2 mm and leaning back 10 degrees reads +3.90, +4.88 and +3.69, where the old reader gave +5.12, +6.25 and +5.00. The new reader is better there, but the reading is still 3.7 to 4.9 degrees off and not flagged.\n5. A flush dens leaning back 10 degrees reads -1.3 to -2.2 on the small body, below the claim's lower bound of 2.3.\n\nSo: at C2 the wall is read partly on the dens, and nothing refuses or flags it. Over my dens cases the error ran from under 1 to 8 degrees, depending on the dens's shape. Pedicle sagittal angles could not be checked, since this cervical phantom's pedicles are refused on both sides with or without the dens.",
            "measured": "Probe: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_1_1/dens_probe.py. Logs in the same folder: thin_x2.log, thick_x2.log, thin_s18.log and tall_*.log.\n\nWall error in degrees, new reader / old reader (a9f21fd), x2 grid, 1 mm slices:\n- no dens: -0.12 / -4.63\n- flush: +2.60 / +1.95\n- recessed 1.5 mm, upright: +8.08 / +7.46\n- recessed 1.5 mm, back 12 degrees: +3.83 / +2.31\n- flush, back 10 degrees: -2.01 / -1.83\n\n2.5 mm slices, same cases: -0.41 / -7.13, +2.31 / -1.13, +7.77 / +4.17, +3.64 / +0.77, -1.29 / -5.38.\n\nsuite18 grid, turned 7 degrees, 1 mm slices:\n- no dens: -0.73 / +1.02\n- flush: +1.61 / +1.75\n- recessed 1.5 mm, back 12 degrees: +2.48 / +3.24\n- flush, back 10 degrees: -2.24 / +1.51\n\nTaller body (20 mm, dens 15 mm), on thin x2, thick x2 and thin suite18:\n- no dens: +0.00, -0.09, -0.28\n- flush, back 10 degrees: -0.79, -0.38, -0.90\n- recessed 2 mm, back 10 degrees: +3.90, +4.88, +3.69 (old +5.12, +6.25, +5.00)\n\nWindow share above the body's top: 35 to 50 per cent of the window and 22 to 41 per cent of the edges. Freedom in the dens cases: 0.21 to 0.84 degrees. No posterior_wall or posterior_wall_free flag in any dens case; the only wall flag in these runs was posterior_wall_free, on the small body with no dens at 2.5 mm.\n\nC2 row errors on thin x2, no dens then flush, recessed and recessed-leaning dens:\n- endplate_lower_incl: -3.37, then -5.86, -11.47, -7.27\n- spinous_angle: +0.54, then +3.31, +8.79, +4.48"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "wall_slope_range reads HiGHS's no-verdict status 4 as an unbounded range, so one speck behind the wall refuses it as 'free over 180 degrees'",
        "severity": "medium",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 573,
        "detail": "wall_slope_range takes only status 2 as 'does not part'. Every other failure becomes an infinite end. I added one voxel of bone one step behind the wall's edge nearest the window's middle (a segmenter's speck; one_voxel.py). The widest parting margin then drops to between 7.8e-9 and 8.4e-8, under WALL_PART_MM. Both range programs come back with status 4, 'HiGHS Status 15: model_status is Unknown; primal_status is Infeasible' (lp_status.py, scipy 1.18.1). The code then returns (-inf, +inf) with parted True, so free is 180 and the wall is refused with 'the lattice leaves the wall's slope free over 180.0 degrees, more than 9'. Both inclinations, both pedicle sagittal angles and the spinous angle go NAN with that false reason. This happens on 6 of the 98 cached vertebrae: straight_lean+5_x5_thin, pitched_down_lean+0_x10_thick, pitched_lean+0_x10_thick, shapes_lean+0_aligned_thick, straight_lean+0_aligned_thick and straight_lean+0_x10_thick. I treated status 4 as not parting and read those six again (status4_fix.py). They read -0.01, +0.22, +0.22, +0.03, +0.04 and +0.22, the same as without the speck, with freedoms of 0.92 to 2.62. Logs: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/review_geometry/ov_thin.log, ov_thick.log, status4_fix.log.",
        "fix": "Treat any status other than 0 and 3 as 'does not part': return NAN, NAN for 2 and 4, and give an infinite end only for status 3. Or, as pedicle.parting does, first solve for the widest margin and call the label parted only where that margin is above WALL_PART_MM. Add a test with one speck behind the wall on the aligned 2.5 mm phantom.",
        "lens": "geometry",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds; I could not refute it. The defect is in the uncommitted change, at morphometry/body.py lines 571 and 573. In wall_slope_range, only status 2 counts as \"does not part\". Every other result that is not a success becomes an infinite end. scipy 1.18.1's HiGHS returns status 4 (\"HiGHS Status 15: model_status is Unknown; primal_status is Infeasible\") when the label fails to part by WALL_PART_MM only at the 1e-6 scale. That is exactly what one bone voxel one step behind the wall's middle edge does. The code then reports the range as -inf..+inf (or -inf..b) with parted True, and read_wall refuses the wall with a false \"free over 180 (or 90.2) degrees\" reason. Since any infinite end makes free at least 90 degrees, more than the 9-degree bound, status 4 always ends in a refusal, never in a wrong slope.\n\nI measured this with my own scripts, not the reviewer's. A widest-margin program on the same centres, solved by highs, highs-ds and highs-ipm, gives margins of 1.85e-11 to 8.45e-8 in all six cases, below WALL_PART_MM (1e-6). By the code's own definition the label does not part, so the right answer is NAN, NAN, which leads to the clean_digitisation fallback.\n\nDownstream, with the speck as the code stands: both end-plate inclinations, the read pedicle sagittal angle and the spinous angle go NAN, under the flags 'posterior_wall', 'spinous' and 'pedicle_left', each giving the 180-degree reason. With status 4 treated as no parting, the readings match the clean vertebra to within 0.05 degrees and none of those flags is raised.\n\nOne small inaccuracy in the claim: on pitched_down_lean+0_x10_thick only the first program returns 4. The second returns 0, even though the widest margin there is 1.85e-11, so the freedom reads 90.2 degrees, not 180. The refusal still happens. That case also shows HiGHS accepting a slightly infeasible point as status 0, which supports the reviewer's second fix: solve for the widest margin first and call the label parted only where that margin is above WALL_PART_MM.\n\nEverything was run on cached phantoms only. Scripts and logs are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_2_0/: speck_status.py, downstream.py, two.log, all.log and downstream.log.",
            "measured": "Probe: speck_status.py adds one bone voxel one step behind the wall edge nearest the window's middle on the midline, logs every linprog status inside body, and solves a widest-margin LP on the same centres. Results over all 98 cached vertebrae (all.log): 6 have status 4 and are refused, and 92 are read. The six are the ones the reviewer named: straight_lean+5_x5_thin, pitched_down_lean+0_x10_thick, pitched_lean+0_x10_thick, shapes_lean+0_aligned_thick, straight_lean+0_aligned_thick and straight_lean+0_x10_thick.\n\n| Case | Range-program statuses | Freedom read | Widest margin |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| pitched_down_lean+0_x10_thick | [4, 0] | 90.2 | 1.85e-11 |\n| pitched_lean+0_x10_thick | [4, 4] | 180.0 | 7.77e-09 |\n| shapes_lean+0_aligned_thick | [4, 4] | 180.0 | 1.28e-08 |\n| straight_lean+0_aligned_thick | [4, 4] | 180.0 | 1.3e-08 |\n| straight_lean+0_x10_thick | [4, 4] | 180.0 | 1.79e-09 |\n| straight_lean+5_x5_thin | [4, 4] | 180.0 | 8.45e-08 |\n\nEvery margin is under WALL_PART_MM (1e-6). Specks whose programs return status 2 (for example the aligned thin cases, statuses [2, 0, 0]) are handled correctly through clean_digitisation.\n\nDownstream (downstream.py, through measure.vertebra_values):\n- straight_lean+5_x5_thin. Clean: endplate_incl -5.73, endplate_lower_incl -4.14, left pedicle sagittal -5.48, spinous 25.54. Speck as written: all NAN, with the flags posterior_wall, spinous and pedicle_left all giving \"free over 180.0\". Speck with status 4 treated as no parting: -5.73, -4.14, -5.48 and 25.53, with no wall flag.\n- straight_lean+0_aligned_thick. Clean: endplate_incl -0.01, endplate_lower_incl -0.00, spinous 17.83. Speck as written: NAN, NAN, NAN. Speck with status 4 treated as no parting: -0.04, -0.04 and 17.87."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The clamp is all or nothing: one voxel among 80 to 240 edges stops the label parting, and the reading falls back to the fit alone, about 1 degree worse",
        "severity": "medium",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 418,
        "detail": "The clamp only applies when every edge pair parts. One speck behind the wall is enough to stop that. When the clamp lets go, the reading becomes the fit alone. On thin slices, one speck takes straight_lean+0_x2_thin and pitched_lean+0_x2_thin from +0.25 to +1.24, straight_lean+5_y12_thin from +0.20 to +1.24, and pitched_down_lean+0_x2_thin from +0.15 to +0.96. That is the near-aligned grid the change was made for, and every one of these is past WALL_TOL_DEG (0.5). On thick slices the suite18 grid goes from +0.25..+0.36 to +1.04..+1.15, and straight_lean-5_x2_thick from +0.45 to +1.01. On the clean cache the fit alone errs up to 1.25 degrees on 1 mm slices and 1.18 on 2.5 mm (medians 0.10 and 0.19). The figures quoted in the posterior_wall docstring, the pedicle module paragraph and the README (0.35 and 0.79) are the clamp's. They hold only for labels that are an exact centre-sampled digitisation, which is what the phantoms are (97 of 98 part). The docstring itself says real labels almost never part. On all 97 parted clean readings the truth lay inside the parted range (range_check.py), so the clamp itself is sound; it is just fragile. Logs: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/review_geometry/ov_thin.log, ov_thick.log, range_check.log.",
        "fix": "Clamp into the range of surfaces that part all but the few centres a least-crossing program puts across. pedicle.parting already does this with its second linear program. Or drop the pairs whose residual marks them before testing whether the label parts. Otherwise, quote the fit-alone figures (1.25 / 1.18) as the accuracy to expect on real masks, and keep 0.35 / 0.79 as the clean-digitisation case.",
        "lens": "geometry",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds, both in the code and in the numbers.\n\n**The code.** In read_wall, `wall_freedom(bone[good], ...)` runs the parting linear programs over every kept edge, meaning every edge within one y-step of the fitted surface. If that program is infeasible, `parted` is False and `b = float(coef[1])`, so the reading is the fit alone. Nothing between the two discards a single bad edge. A speck one step behind a wall edge becomes that scan line's new edge. Its residual is about the original edge's residual minus one step. When the original edge lies on the positive side of the fit, which is most of the time, the speck stays among the kept edges and the program has no solution.\n\n**What I measured.** All on the 98 clean cached phantoms, with my own probes in verify_3_0, not the reviewer's script.\n\n- **The fit alone on the clean cache.** It errs at most 1.24 degrees on 1 mm slices and 1.18 on 2.5 mm, with medians 0.10 and 0.19. The clamped reading errs at most 0.35 and 0.79 (medians 0.08 and 0.19). The fit alone is past 0.5 on 4 of the 49 thin vertebrae and 9 of the 49 thick ones. The worst cases are pitched_lean+0_x2_thin and shapes_lean+0_suite18_thick. Log: fit_alone.log.\n- **One speck at six edges spread over the window, per vertebra.** I added one voxel of bone one step behind each edge, the real code then read the wall, and I counted only vertebrae whose clean label parts.\n  - Thin slices: 168 of 288 specks stop the parting, 17 of those leave the reading past 0.5, and the largest error is 1.30 against 0.35 clean.\n  - Thick slices: 150 of 294 specks stop the parting, 27 past 0.5, largest 1.30 against 0.79 clean.\n  - A single speck also made the wall refused 4 times.\n  - Logs: speck_all.log, summarised by summarise.py.\n- **The reviewer's examples reproduce.**\n  - straight_lean+0_x2_thin and pitched_lean+0_x2_thin go from +0.25 to between +1.21 and +1.26.\n  - straight_lean+5_y12_thin goes from +0.20 to between +1.20 and +1.30.\n  - shapes_lean+0_suite18_thick goes from +0.34 to between +1.10 and +1.30.\n  - straight_lean-5_x2_thick goes from +0.45 to between +0.77 and +1.06.\n  - In every case the speck's edge was among the kept edges whenever the parting failed. Log: speck.log.\n\n**Small inaccuracies.**\n- The edge counts are 72 to 99 on thick slices and 200 to 270 on thin ones, not \"80 to 240\".\n- The fit-alone maximum on thin slices is 1.24, not 1.25.\n- The docstring does not say that \"real labels\" almost never part. It says that labels with their boundary voxels flipped, or with a ragged edge, almost never part and that the fit then stands alone. It also gives figures for altered labels, up to 1.64 and 3.03 degrees.\n\nSo the fallback is partly disclosed in the docstring. But the README and the pedicle module paragraph lead with 0.35 and 0.79 as the reading's accuracy, without saying those figures need a label that is a clean digitisation, and one voxel is enough to lose the clamp. The claim's substance stands: the clamp is all or nothing, one speck usually breaks it, and the reading then errs by about 1 degree more, past WALL_TOL_DEG, on the grids near the vertebra's frame that the change was made for.\n\nAll files are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_3_0/.",
            "measured": "On the 98 clean cached phantoms, the fit alone (info fitted_deg) errs at most 1.24 degrees thin and 1.18 thick (medians 0.10 and 0.19); the clamped reading errs at most 0.35 and 0.79. With one bone voxel added one step behind a wall edge, at six edges spread over the window per vertebra and counting only labels that part when clean: on thin slices 168 of 288 specks stop the parting, 17 of those land past 0.5, and the largest error is 1.30; on thick slices 150 of 294 stop it, 27 land past 0.5, and the largest is 1.30. A single speck made the wall refused 4 times. In speck.log the speck's own edge was among the kept edges (within a step of the fit) in every case where the parting failed. Files: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_3_0/fit_alone.py, fit_alone.log, speck.py, speck.log, speck_all.log, summarise.py."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The gate is centred on a canal line that a notch leans 14 to 29 degrees, so it shuts out the wall's lower end and lets the notch floor in",
        "severity": "medium",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 69,
        "detail": "The comment on WALL_GATE_SHARE says the reach lies 'past the line's error'. Here it does not. On the 8x8x5 notch on thick slices (suite grid), anatomy.wall_slope's line leans -24.90 degrees, with y0 -3.18 at z0 2.93. Over the window's lowest 2 mm the line stands at +1.45 while the wall's edges are at -6.05: a 7.5 mm error against a reach of 4.81. The wall's lower end falls outside the gate, while the notch floor at about -1 between z -4 and +4 falls inside. That gives the -2.98 reading. On the 8x6x5 notch on thick slices the line leans -14.01 and the reading is +29.49. I opened the gate about a level line at the canal's median front instead (v.wall_slope 0, v.wall_y0 v.wall): it reads +0.37 and parts. A level line is not a general fix, though. On the 8x8x5 notch it makes things worse (+29.78), and on the x2 grid the line had already been reset to 0 by WALL_SLOPE_MAX. So the gate is one of two causes, and the trimmed share of the high finding is the other. Probes: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/review_geometry/diag_gate.py (dg_thick_885.log, dg_thick_865.log).",
        "fix": "Stop counting on the canal line's lean inside this reader. Either refuse where the fitted wall at the midline leaves the gate's band inside the window, or read the gate from each full ring's own anterior wall at its z instead of the Theil-Sen line, so that a notch's slabs only widen the gate where the notch is. Then fix the WALL_GATE_SHARE comment to say what the reach does and does not cover.",
        "lens": "geometry",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds. I reproduced it with my own probe, which builds the phantom the way the test helper `built` does. The probe sorts every candidate edge in the band and window by where it lies in the phantom's own frame: the wall, the notch floor, or other. It then reads the wall again with the gate centred on the phantom's true wall line. The gate in wall_edges is centred on anatomy's canal line (square_to_wall, v.wall_y0) at a reach of WALL_GATE_SHARE times the canal depth. Where a notch pulls that line forward, the comment \"past the line's error\" is false.\n\nOne detail of the mechanism is wrong. On the 8x6x5 thick case the gate keeps every wall edge, so the wall's lower end is not shut out there. The failure in that case comes from the notch floor being let in.\n\nA gate centred on the wall is not a full fix either: on the 8x8x4 thick notch the trimmed fit is the cause, which is the reviewer's \"second cause\". Neither point overturns the defect.",
            "measured": "Probes are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_4_0/: gate_probe.py and flags_probe.py, with logs p_*.log and f_*.log.\n\n- **8x8x5 thick, suite grid:** reads -2.98, as the reviewer says. The canal line leans -24.90 deg. At z -7 it stands 7.63 mm in front of the true wall, against a reach of 4.81.\n  - None of the 26 wall edges below z -2 is inside the gate (0/13, 0/8, 0/5). All 27 notch-floor edges are inside.\n  - The fit keeps 8 wall edges and 21 notch edges.\n  - With the gate on the true wall line it reads +0.67.\n  - measures gives endplate_incl +4.44 and endplate_lower_incl +1.62 against a truth of 0, with no posterior_wall flag.\n- **8x6x5 thick, suite grid:** reads +29.49, as the reviewer says. The line leans -14.01 deg.\n  - All 51 wall edges are inside the gate, so the lower end is not shut out here. All 26 notch edges are inside too.\n  - The fit keeps 15 wall and 9 notch edges.\n  - With the gate on the true wall line it reads +0.37.\n  - measures gives endplate_incl -29.29 and endplate_lower_incl -29.18, with no flag.\n- **8x6x5 thin, suite grid:** reads -29.15. The line leans -23.56 deg.\n  - Only 1 of 27 wall edges at z -7.1 and 16 of 29 at z -5.1 are inside the gate. All 58 notch edges are inside.\n  - With the gate on the true wall line it reads -0.18.\n- **8x8x5 thick, x2 grid:** the line is level, reset to 0 by WALL_SLOPE_MAX, but it stands 4.4 to 4.9 mm in front of the wall. It reads -29.73; with the gate on the true wall line it reads -0.15. So the line's offset does harm as well as its lean.\n- **8x8x4 thick, suite grid:** reads -4.45. With the gate on the true wall line it reads +23.44, because the notch, which holds 27 of 80 edges, falls inside even a gate centred on the wall. In this case the trimmed fit is the cause.\n- **The suite's own 5x6x4 thin notch:** reads -0.20. The gate already drops 2 of 27 wall edges at z -7.1, where the line stands 4.73 mm off the wall against a reach of 4.75."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "On cervical bodies the 6 mm band reaches the pedicle roots and biases the wall",
        "severity": "medium",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 443,
        "detail": "The band's half-width is min(6, a quarter of the canal's width). From C3 to C7 the canal (about 24 mm) is wider than the body (15 to 20 mm), so the cap of 6 mm sets the band. Its edge then lies within a couple of millimetres of the body's posterolateral corners, where the converging pedicles join the body. Phantom C5: W 17, D 16, canal 24, pedicles converging 45 degrees, rooted in the body's upper half. On a 0.4x0.4x0.6 mm grid with an upright wall the reading is -0.46 / -0.44 off (grids x2 / suite18) at the 6 mm cap. Capped at 4 mm it is -0.31 / -0.24, and at 3 mm -0.01 / -0.11. With the pedicles moved to the lower half (ped_z -2) the 6 mm reading becomes +0.12 / -0.02, so the bias follows where the pedicle roots sit. On 0.8x0.8x1 mm slices with the wall leaned 5 degrees either way the errors are -0.76, -1.50, -1.13 and -0.87, against the lumbar cache's 0.35. On 2.5 mm slices they reach -1.40 / -1.41. None of the 1 mm readings parts the label, and their freedom reads 0.79 to 1.81, so none is flagged. A thoracic T8-sized phantom (band 4.2 to 4.4 mm) stays within 0.48 on 1 mm slices. Logs: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/review_robustness/cerv_thin.log, cerv_thick.log, cerv_fine.log, cerv_band_thin.log, cerv_pedicle_fine.log, thor_thin.log.",
        "fix": "Cap the band by the body's own width at its back, not only by the canal's. For example, take half no wider than about a third of the body's half-width, read from B within a few millimetres in front of the canal's line. Or leave out scan lines whose bone runs on behind the wall's line (the pedicle roots). Add a cervical phantom with converging pedicles to the suite.",
        "lens": "robustness",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds on phantoms. The code matches it: body.py line 443 sets half = min(WALL_BAND_MAX_MM, 0.25 * the median ring width). On the reviewer's C5 (W 17, D 16, canal 24, pedicles at 45 degrees) the rings measure 26 to 28 mm wide, so every reading ran at the 6.00 mm cap. I reproduced the reviewer's numbers exactly. On 1 mm slices: -0.76, -1.50, -1.13 and -0.87, with freedom 1.81 / 0.93 / 0.79 / 0.93, none parted and none flagged. On 2.5 mm slices: -1.40 and -1.41. On the 0.4 mm grid: -0.46 at ped_z +2 and +0.12 at ped_z -2.\n\nI tied the bias to the pedicle roots with a control the reviewer did not run. I drew the same phantom with each pedicle clipped wherever it stands outside the body within 7 mm of the midline, and changed nothing else. The phantom's body is an ellipse, so its back surface at x = 6 lies 2.3 mm in front of the midline wall. The pedicle's medial edge comes out behind that surface from about x = 5.2 mm, inside the band. In the original label, the edges beyond 5 mm in the pedicle's half of the window lie 0.1 to 1.3 mm behind the fitted surface. Clipped, they lie within about 0.1 mm of it, and the inner edges are unchanged.\n\nThe readings move with the clip, and the sign follows where the pedicles sit:\n- 1 mm slices, ped_z +2: the original reads 0.17 to 0.95 degrees more negative than the clipped one in all 12 cases (mean 0.48). At ped_z -2 it reads more positive in 10 of 12 (mean +0.43).\n- 2.5 mm slices: the shift reaches 1.36 degrees. It is negative in 10 of 12 at ped_z +2 and positive in 8 of 12 at ped_z -2.\n- 0.4 mm grid: the clip takes x0 and x2 to within 0.24, for example -0.46 to -0.24, -0.19 to 0.00 and +0.26 to 0.00.\n- Largest error: 1.50 / 0.89 on 1 mm slices for the original against 0.57 / 0.86 clipped, and 1.42 against 0.91 on 2.5 mm slices.\n\nTwo corrections to how the reviewer frames it:\n- The reviewer's own cap test on 1 mm slices was confounded. A 3 mm cap still errs by about 1 degree because the narrower band leaves the slope freer (2 to 7.6 degrees). The clip is the cleaner test.\n- Not all of the cervical excess over the lumbar cache's 0.35 / 0.79 is the band. With the pedicles clipped, readings still err by up to 0.86 on 1 mm slices and 0.91 on 2.5 mm. The pedicle share is about 0.5 degrees on average and up to 1.4.\n\nAll of this is phantom geometry. Whether real cervical masks carry pedicle bone behind the wall within 6 mm of the midline is not measured. The implementer's open items already say the band has not been looked at on cervical bodies, and the comment on WALL_BAND_MAX_MM says its purpose is to keep off the pedicle roots, which this phantom shows it does not do. Probes and logs are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_5_0/ (common.py, probe_clip.py, clip_thin.log, clip_thick.log, clip_fine_up.log, clip_fine_down.log). I stopped the 0.4 mm runs after the x0 and x2 tilts.",
            "measured": "Reviewer's C5 phantom, half band 6.00 mm in every case, error in degrees at cap 6, original vs clipped pedicles.\n1 mm slices, ped_z +2: x0 lean 0 -0.26/+0.01; x0 lean+5 -0.76/-0.10; x0 lean-5 -1.50/-0.55; x2 lean 0 -0.02/+0.15; x2 lean+5 -0.72/-0.22; x2 lean-5 -0.28/+0.07; x5 lean 0 +0.25/+0.53; x5 lean+5 -0.60/+0.01; x5 lean-5 -0.89/-0.42; suite18 lean 0 -0.59/-0.26; suite18 lean+5 -1.13/-0.57; suite18 lean-5 -0.87/-0.30.\n1 mm slices, ped_z -2: original +0.21, +0.44, -0.22, +0.09, +0.45, +0.31, +0.89, +0.45, +0.01, +0.71, +0.30, +0.46; clipped +0.02, -0.27, -0.86, +0.10, +0.50, -0.34, +0.23, -0.00, -0.29, -0.06, -0.36, +0.23.\n2.5 mm slices: largest error 1.41 (ped_z +2) and 1.42 (ped_z -2) for the original, 0.91 for both clipped.\n0.4 mm grid, original/clipped: ped_z +2 x0 -0.19/0.00, -0.38/-0.16, -0.16/+0.05; x2 -0.46/-0.24, -0.13/+0.15, -0.18/-0.11. ped_z -2 x0 +0.13/0.00, +0.05/-0.13, +0.26/0.00; x2 +0.12/-0.19, +0.28/+0.13, +0.20/-0.03.\nMean residual of the edges beyond 5 mm in the pedicle's half: original -0.14 to -1.30 mm, clipped -0.22 to +0.15 mm."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Where the label does not part, the freedom is no bound, so refusal and flag rarely fire",
        "severity": "medium",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 556,
        "detail": "When no surface parts the label, wall_freedom takes the range from clean_digitisation: the lattice a perfect digitisation of the fitted surface would leave. That range measures only quantisation around the fit, never how far the label's edges lie from the surface family, so the truth often falls outside it. The docstring says it 'bounds the reading', and WALL_FREEDOM_DEG (refuse) and WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG (flag) both rely on it. Yet the judge's own notes show real-mask-like labels almost never part: flips and ragged edges part 0 to 2 of 49. Cases measured, none refused or flagged: cervical C5 on 0.4 mm, err -0.46 with freedom 0.04 (range -0.50 to -0.46, truth -0.03); C5 on 1 mm with the wall leaned -5, err -1.50 with freedom 0.93; C2 with its dens, +3.83 with freedom 0.34 and +8.08 with 0.62; the cached straight_lean+5_y12_thin with 1 per cent of the voxels in the 3 mm in front of the wall removed at random, +1.21 with freedom 0.28. Under heavier random damage (fuzz.py, 882 trials, no exception raised) readings 6 to 10 degrees off are accepted with freedom 0.3 to 0.5, for example straight_lean-5_x2_thin at -10.16 with 0.31. Logs: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/review_robustness/cerv_fine.log, cerv_band_thin.log, dens_more_thin.log, specks_0.01.log, fuzz_0.log to fuzz_3.log.",
        "fix": "Do not report the synthetic range as the reading's freedom. Either compute the range on the label itself with a slack (the slopes of surfaces that part bone from air to within, say, half a step, or that leave at most the trimmed share unparted), or mark a reading that does not part as unbounded, with its own flag. Correct the docstring's 'It bounds the reading', and the README, accordingly.",
        "lens": "robustness",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds. In read_wall, when the label does not part, wall_freedom takes the range from clean_digitisation. That function makes synthetic bone and air centres on either side of the fitted surface, on the edges' own scan lines. So the range sits around the fit and measures only how finely the lattice pins that fit. It says nothing about how far the label's real edges lie from the surface family, so the truth can fall outside it. Two statements depend on it being a bound: the docstring's \"It bounds the reading\" and wall_flags' message \"the angles taken against the wall may be off by up to half that\". Both rest on that range, and so do the WALL_FREEDOM_DEG refusal and the WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG flag. Where the label parts, the range is a real bound under clean digitisation, and I measured that it is. Where it does not part, it is not, and the damaged labels I tried almost never part. The judge's freedom_calib.txt shows the same pattern: err <= synth/2 on only 23 of 49 flipped-voxel labels and 11 of 49 ragged ones on thin slices. My measurements are under measured.",
            "measured": "I ran body.posterior_wall from the working tree on the 98 cached L3s, clean and altered by the judge's robust.altered. Scripts: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_6_0/coverage.py and /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_6_0/table.py; output in cov_0.json and cov_1.json in the same folder.\n\nLabels that part (the range is a real bound):\n- Clean: truth inside [free_lo, free_hi] on 48 of 48 thin and 49 of 49 thick; |err| <= free on all of them.\n- Holes: truth inside on 41 of 42 thin and 46 of 46 thick.\n- Even here, |err| <= free/2, the bound the flag's message states, holds on only 36 of 48 clean thin and 40 of 49 clean thick.\n\nLabels that do not part (the range is not a bound):\n- flips_0.2 (0 of 49 part on either slice thickness):\n  - thin: truth inside 21 of 49, |err| <= free/2 on 23; flagged 2, refused 0.\n  - thick: truth inside 31 of 49, |err| <= free/2 on 36; flagged 9, 4 unflagged readings over 1.5 degrees, worst 2.23 at a freedom of 1.80.\n- ragged_0.3_3 (0 of 49 part on thin slices, 2 of 49 on thick):\n  - thin: truth inside 7 of 49, |err| <= free/2 on 11; 9 unflagged readings over 1.5 degrees, max 2.56.\n  - thick, not parted: truth inside 16 of 47; 9 unflagged readings over 1.5 degrees, max 3.97 at a freedom of 0.99.\n  - Part of the ragged error is the label's own lean, which the docstring already concedes no reader of the label can tell from the wall.\n- Refused: 0 in every set.\n\nCervical cases, rerun independently with /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_6_0/cervical_one.py (logs cerv_fine_x2.log and cerv_thin_x2.log in that folder):\n- C5 on 0.4 mm voxels, grid x2, lean 0: parted False, range -0.50 to -0.46, truth -0.03, err -0.46, freedom 0.04. This is the reviewer's figure exactly.\n- C5 on 1 mm slices, grid x2, lean 5: parted False, range 8.88 to 9.61, truth 9.89, err -0.72, freedom 0.73.\n\nI did not rerun the reviewer's heavy fuzz, C2 dens or 1 per cent speck numbers. I only read their logs, which match what the claim quotes. On the claim's main point, my measurements agree with the reviewer."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The free-wall flag promises an error of at most half the freedom, which the clamp does not give",
        "severity": "medium",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 597,
        "detail": "wall_flags' docstring and the flag text it writes (which measure.rows_of puts out as a flag:posterior_wall_free row) say \"the angles taken against the wall may be off by up to half that\". The comment on WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG (line 79, \"half of it past the suite's 1.5 degrees\") rests on the same half. The code does not support this. read_wall clamps b into [b_lo, b_hi] but does not centre it. If the truth also lies in that range, the error can reach the whole width, not half of it. Measured with body.posterior_wall as it stands, over the 98 cached clean vertebrae (scratch/wall/review_words/clean_stats.py): the error is more than half the freedom on 22 of 98 (13 thin, 9 thick), for example straight_lean+5_x2_thick at -0.79 with freedom 1.46 and straight_lean+0_x2_thin at 0.25 with 0.44. It is never more than the full freedom (0 of 98). Among readings that the flag would mark (freedom over 3), the implementer's alteration rows (impl/robust_0.json and robust_1.json, counted by review_words/half_free.py) give 6 past half. Examples: ragged_0.5_4 on straight_lean+0_x2_thin reads +4.96 off with freedom 4.02, where the flag would promise at most 2.0; lip_top_1.5 on straight_lean-5_x2_thick reads -7.78 off with freedom 5.22.",
        "fix": "Say \"may be off by up to that\": on a clean digitisation the reading and the truth both lie in the range. Then restate the threshold's reason in the WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG comment, since a 3-degree range lets the angles err by up to 3, twice the suite's 1.5. Or lower the threshold to 1.5 if the flag was meant to mark readings that could fail the suite.",
        "lens": "words",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds, both in the code and in what I measured. read_wall (body.py lines 417-419) clamps b into [b_lo, b_hi] with min(max(coef[1], b_lo), b_hi). It does not centre b, and it does not clamp at all where the label does not part. The truth of a clean digitisation lies somewhere in the same range, so the error is bounded only by the whole width. Nothing in the code supports \"up to half that\". That wording appears in three places: wall_flags' docstring (line 597), the flag text it writes (line 603, which measure.rows_of puts out as flag:posterior_wall_free), and the WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG comment (line 79, \"half of it past the suite's 1.5 degrees\").\n\nThe parted case works as the reviewer says. On every clean label that parts, the truth lies inside the range: 48 of 48 thin, 49 of 49 thick. The reading often sits at one end of the range, where the clamp has acted: 22 thin and 8 thick readings sit within 1 percent of an end.\n\nOn flagged readings the promise fails outright, because the flagged labels did not part, so nothing clamps them. Both examples read past the full freedom, not only past half of it, while the flag text says \"up to half that\". In fairness, the 3 clean flagged readings (all at 2.5 mm) err within half: -0.12 against 2.27, +0.68 against 1.64 and +0.00 against 3.94.\n\nOne correction to the proposed fix: \"may be off by up to that\" holds only where the label parts. The two altered flagged readings did not part and erred past the full freedom, so the new text should be limited to labels that part.",
            "measured": "All runs used body.posterior_wall as it stands in the working tree, with the cached _posterior_wall cleared before each read, scored against score.wall_truth_in_frame. Scripts: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_7_0/half_claim.py (log half_claim.log, rows half_claim.json) and /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_7_0/altered_flagged.py (log altered_flagged.log).\n\nClean cache, 98 vertebrae:\n- 1 mm slices: 49 read, 48 parted, truth inside the range on 48 of 48. The error passes half the freedom on 13 and the full freedom on 0. |err|/free: max 0.72, median 0.35. The reading's place in the range runs from 0.00 to 1.00 (median 0.53), and 22 readings sit within 1 percent of an end. Example: shapes_lean+4_aligned_thin errs -0.18 with freedom 0.25; the reading sits at 0.00 of the range and the truth at 0.72.\n- 2.5 mm slices: 49 read, 49 parted, truth inside on 49 of 49. The error passes half the freedom on 9 and the full freedom on 0. |err|/free: max 0.75, median 0.17. 8 readings sit within 1 percent of an end. Example: straight_lean+5_suite18_thick errs -0.58 with freedom 0.77; the reading sits at 0.00 and the truth at 0.75. Both counts match the reviewer's 13 and 9.\n- The three flagged clean readings (freedom over 3, all at 2.5 mm) err within half: shapes_lean+4_aligned_thick -0.12 against half 2.27, shapes_lean+4_x5_thick +0.68 against 1.64, straight_lean+5_x5_thick +0.00 against 3.94.\n\nAltered labels, re-read through judge/robust.altered:\n- ragged_0.5_4 on straight_lean+0_x2_thin: err +4.96, freedom 4.02, half 2.01. The label does not part, the range is [+1.93, +5.96] and the truth is -0.07.\n- lip_top_1.5 on straight_lean-5_x2_thick: err -7.78, freedom 5.22, half 2.61. The label does not part, the range is [-19.32, -14.10] and the truth is -7.34.\n- wall_flags writes, for both: \"the angles taken against the wall may be off by up to half that\"."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The band is twice as wide as its constants and the docstring say",
        "severity": "medium",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 67,
        "detail": "The comments say WALL_BAND_SHARE is \"the band across the midline, as a share of the canal's width\" and WALL_BAND_MAX_MM (line 68) is \"no wider than this, so a wide canal's band does not reach a pedicle's root\". posterior_wall's docstring (lines 337-338) says the band is \"WALL_BAND_SHARE of the canal's width, at most WALL_BAND_MAX_MM\". The code uses the value as a half-width: wall_window sets half = min(6, 0.25 * width), and wall_window and wall_edges keep |x - xc| <= half. So the band is 2 * half wide: up to 12 mm, half the canal's width. Measured: half is 5.58 to 6.00 mm on the 98 cached vertebrae and 5.72 on the suite's notch phantom, so the band is 11.2 to 12.0 mm wide. The pedicle-root reasoning is made for a 6 mm band, and the implementer's open item (\"capped only at 6 mm\") repeats the misreading, which matters for the cervical bodies not yet looked at. wall_window's own docstring correctly says \"half-width\". In the same docstring (line 438), \"the middle WALL_HEIGHT_SHARE of the body's points in the band\" is not what the code does either: it trims the z range of those points by 20 per cent at each end, it does not take quantiles of the points.",
        "fix": "Use half-width in both comments and in posterior_wall's docstring, for example \"the band reaches WALL_BAND_SHARE of the canal's width either side of the midline, at most WALL_BAND_MAX_MM\". Check that the pedicle-root reason still holds at 12 mm. In wall_window write \"the middle WALL_HEIGHT_SHARE of the height those points span\".",
        "lens": "words",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds. In body.py, wall_window sets half = min(WALL_BAND_MAX_MM, WALL_BAND_SHARE * median canal width). Both wall_window and wall_edges then keep points with |x - xc| <= half, so `half` is a half-width. The canal width (CanalSlab.width, from scanimport.geometry.canal_walls) is the full width, right surface minus left. So the band is 2*half wide: up to 12 mm, half the canal's width. The comments disagree with this. The comment on WALL_BAND_SHARE (line 67) calls it \"the band across the midline, as a share of the canal's width\". The comment on WALL_BAND_MAX_MM (line 68) says \"no wider than this\". The posterior_wall docstring (lines 337-338) says \"WALL_BAND_SHARE of the canal's width, at most WALL_BAND_MAX_MM\". All three describe a band a quarter of the canal wide and at most 6 mm wide. Elsewhere the code is labelled correctly: wall_window's docstring says \"the band's half-width\", and the info key is half_band_mm. The judge's proposed.py header also says \"the band's half-width a quarter of the canal's width ... at most 6 mm\". So the code does what was intended, and only the implementer's words are wrong.\n\nThe second point also holds, though it is minor. The wall_window docstring says the window is \"the middle WALL_HEIGHT_SHARE of the body's points in the band\". The code instead trims 20 per cent off each end of the z range those points span. It takes no quantiles of the points.\n\nBehaviour is unaffected; this is a defect in the words only. The pedicle-root reason still holds at the real 12 mm width. The band reaches at most 6 mm either side of the midline, and the canal's medial walls stand about 11.2 to 12.3 mm out.",
            "measured": "Probes: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_8_0/band.py (the cache) and /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_8_0/notch.py (the suite's notch phantom, built by test_morphometry.built). Both call body.wall_window and body.wall_edges as they stand in the working tree.\n\nOn the 98 cached vertebrae:\n- Canal width is 22.34 to 24.51 mm.\n- The code's `half` is 5.58 to 6.00 mm, capped at 6 on 14 of them.\n- The full band is 11.17 to 12.00 mm, which is 0.490 to 0.500 of the canal's width.\n- The kept edges themselves lie from -5.99 to +6.00 mm about the midline and span 10.32 to 11.98 mm across x.\n\nOn the notch phantom (notch_w 5, notch_h 6, notch_d 4, default grid):\n- Canal width is 22.89 mm, half is 5.72 mm and the full band is 11.45 mm (0.500 of the width).\n- 217 edges span -5.66 to +5.70 mm (11.36 mm), and the info record's half_band_mm is 5.72.\n- The canal's medial walls stand about +-11.45 mm from the midline, so the 6 mm reach does not get to a pedicle's root.\n\nThe window against quantiles of the points: on the cache, the window's lower bound minus the 20 per cent quantile of the band points' z runs from -1.01 to +0.41 mm. Its upper bound minus the 80 per cent quantile runs from -0.45 to +1.44 mm. So the window is the middle 60 per cent of the height those points span, not the middle 60 per cent of the points."
          }
        ]
      },
