            "reason": "The claim holds in substance. I read the code and measured it on my own phantoms, with a dens drawn independently of the reviewer's: an elliptic cylinder 9 mm across and 10 mm deep, standing on the body's top.\n\nThe code path:\n- wall_window (body.py line 435 on) sets the window to the middle WALL_HEIGHT_SHARE (60 per cent) of the z range of body points within the band.\n- At C2 the body cut (anatomy.body_in_front, everything in front of the canal line) holds the dens. So the window runs up onto the dens, and the gated edges are taken there too.\n- measure.SKIP leaves out only C1 and the sacrum. no_dens_plate drops only the upper-plate keys in DENS.\n- So C2 keeps endplate_lower_incl, spinous_angle and pedicle_angle_sagittal. All three are taken against this wall: inclinations, posterior.spinous_angle, and pedicle.wall_reading, which goes through BODY.posterior_wall.\n- Neither the refusal nor the flag added by the change fires.\n\nWhat I measured. Scripts and logs are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_1_0/ (probe_c2.py, probe_c2_rows.py, c2_thin_H14_x2.log, c2_thick_H14.log, c2_thin_H22.log, c2_rows_thin_H14.log).\n- Cervical C2 at the reviewer's size (body 14 mm), 1 mm slices, grid tilted 2 degrees. Without a dens the wall is off by -0.12. With a dens it is off by +2.62 (back flush, upright), -2.49 (flush, leaning back 12), +5.98 (1 mm in front, upright), +1.82 (1 mm in front, leaning 12) and -6.94 (1 mm behind, leaning 12).\n- The same body on 2.5 mm slices: up to -7.10 on grid x2 and up to -9.16 on suite18.\n- In all of these the edges' span roughly doubles (8 to 16 mm), the freedom stays at 0.17 to 2.10 degrees, and no posterior_wall or posterior_wall_free flag is set. The only flag is posterior_wall_free, set on the dens-free baseline itself on thick slices.\n- The kept rows move one for one with the wall (thin, x2). endplate_lower_incl's error is -3.37 without the dens, -9.37 with the dens 1 mm in front and upright, and +3.54 with it 1 mm behind and leaning 12. spinous_angle's error goes from +0.54 to +6.68 and -6.23. Again no wall flag.\n- On this cervical phantom the pedicles' sagittal angle is NAN for reasons of its own, with or without a dens. So I could not show it carrying the error, though the code takes it against the same wall.\n\nWhere the claim overstates:\n- \"Mostly on the dens\" depends on how tall the body is next to the dens. The dens holds 46 to 49 per cent of the window with the reviewer's 14 mm body under a 14 mm dens, and 28 to 31 per cent with a taller 22 mm body.\n- With the taller body the errors shrink to 0.2 to 3.5 degrees, still unflagged: +3.37, +3.51 and +3.04 with the dens's back 1 mm in front of the wall, and 0.9 to 1.6 flush and upright.\n- \"The change makes C2 worse\" does not hold across shapes. The old reader used the same 60 per cent window of B's z range and errs at C2 too. Its errors were sometimes smaller (thick suite18, dens 1 mm behind, leaning 12: old -1.69, new -9.16) and sometimes larger (thick x2, flush, leaning 12: old -5.70, new -1.75; no dens, thin x2: old -4.63, new -0.12).\n- So the window's reach onto the dens was there before this change. The rewritten posterior_wall and its new flags carry it on, and catch none of it.\n\nThe finding stands as a real, unflagged defect at C2 in the changed code. Its reach depends on the body-to-dens proportion and the dens's offset, not mostly on the dens in every case.",
            "measured": "Phantom C2 (W 17, D 16, canal 24, with C3 and C4), grid not turned, dens an elliptic cylinder (half-widths 4.5 across and 5 front to back, 14 mm tall). Wall error in degrees, new reader (old reader in brackets):\n- Body 14 mm, 1 mm slices, grid x2. No dens -0.12 (-4.63). Dens back flush, upright +2.62 (+1.78). Flush, leaning 12: -2.49 (-1.90). 1 mm in front, upright: +5.98 (+5.92). 1 mm in front, leaning 12: +1.82 (+0.26). 1 mm behind, leaning 12: -6.94 (-2.05).\n- Body 14 mm, 2.5 mm slices. Grid x2: +2.49, -1.75, +6.37, +2.22, -7.10. Suite18: +1.33, -4.11, +6.05, +0.92, -9.16.\n- Body 22 mm, 1 mm slices, grids x0, x2 and suite18: from -3.17 to +3.51.\n- No posterior_wall or posterior_wall_free flag in any dens case. The edges' span goes from 8 to 16 mm at 14 mm and from 11-13 to 20-21 mm at 22 mm. The dens holds 0.46 to 0.49 of the window at 14 mm and 0.28 to 0.31 at 22 mm.\n- C2 rows from measure.vertebra_values (thin, x2). endplate_lower_incl error: -3.37 (no dens), -9.37 (1 mm in front, upright), +3.54 (1 mm behind, leaning 12). spinous_angle error: +0.54, +6.68, -6.23. The pedicles' sagittal angle is NAN for reasons of its own on this phantom."
          },
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds. I rebuilt the phantoms and got the same numbers. Some of its wording overstates the defect.\n\nWhat the code does. measure.SKIP is (\"C1\", \"sacrum\"), so C2 is built and measured. Its body is anatomy.body_in_front, everything connected in front of the canal line, so the dens is part of B. measure.no_dens_plate sets the DENS keys to NaN, but that list has no endplate_lower_incl, no pedicle_angle_sagittal* and no spinous_angle. All three are still read against body.posterior_wall. wall_window takes the middle 60 per cent of the z range of B's midline points, which at C2 runs from the lower plate to the tip of the dens. That window rule is the old reader's, carried over unchanged from a9f21fd. So the defect was inherited, not introduced, but the new reader keeps it, and its flags do not cover it.\n\nWhat I measured. My own probe (verify_1_1/dens_probe.py) used the reviewer's cervical sizes, a dens 9 mm across and 14 mm tall, and C2 built as measure_volume builds it. On the x2 grid, 1 mm slices:\n- no dens: -0.12\n- dens flush and upright: +2.60\n- base 1.5 mm in front of the wall, upright: +8.08\n- the same, leaning back 12 degrees: +3.83\n- On 2.5 mm slices the same three dens cases read +2.31, +7.77 and +3.64.\nThese are identical to the reviewer's logs. The edges' span goes from 8.1 to 16.1 mm. Freedom is 0.3 to 0.8 degrees, so no dens case is refused or flagged in measure_volume's C2 rows. spinous_angle follows the wall: +0.54 with no dens, then +3.31, +8.79 and +4.48.\n\nCorrections to the claim:\n1. \"Mostly on the dens\" goes too far. 35 to 50 per cent of the window and 22 to 41 per cent of the kept edges lie above the body's top.\n2. \"Proud\" is the wrong word. The reviewer's back +1.5 places the dens's base 1.5 mm in front of the wall, recessed. Leaning back 12 degrees, it is about 1.5 mm proud only at the tip.\n3. \"The change makes the reading worse\" is true only in some cases. On the x2 grid it is worse in every dens case. On suite18 with the grid turned 7 degrees it is mixed: flush 1.61 new against 1.75 old, recessed and leaning back 12 degrees 2.48 against 3.24, flush leaning back 10 degrees -2.24 against +1.51.\n4. On a taller body, closer to C2's own (20 mm, dens 15 mm), a dens flush and leaning back 10 degrees reads -0.79, -0.38 and -0.90, so the error is under a degree. A base recessed 2 mm and leaning back 10 degrees reads +3.90, +4.88 and +3.69, where the old reader gave +5.12, +6.25 and +5.00. The new reader is better there, but the reading is still 3.7 to 4.9 degrees off and not flagged.\n5. A flush dens leaning back 10 degrees reads -1.3 to -2.2 on the small body, below the claim's lower bound of 2.3.\n\nSo: at C2 the wall is read partly on the dens, and nothing refuses or flags it. Over my dens cases the error ran from under 1 to 8 degrees, depending on the dens's shape. Pedicle sagittal angles could not be checked, since this cervical phantom's pedicles are refused on both sides with or without the dens.",
            "measured": "Probe: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_1_1/dens_probe.py. Logs in the same folder: thin_x2.log, thick_x2.log, thin_s18.log and tall_*.log.\n\nWall error in degrees, new reader / old reader (a9f21fd), x2 grid, 1 mm slices:\n- no dens: -0.12 / -4.63\n- flush: +2.60 / +1.95\n- recessed 1.5 mm, upright: +8.08 / +7.46\n- recessed 1.5 mm, back 12 degrees: +3.83 / +2.31\n- flush, back 10 degrees: -2.01 / -1.83\n\n2.5 mm slices, same cases: -0.41 / -7.13, +2.31 / -1.13, +7.77 / +4.17, +3.64 / +0.77, -1.29 / -5.38.\n\nsuite18 grid, turned 7 degrees, 1 mm slices:\n- no dens: -0.73 / +1.02\n- flush: +1.61 / +1.75\n- recessed 1.5 mm, back 12 degrees: +2.48 / +3.24\n- flush, back 10 degrees: -2.24 / +1.51\n\nTaller body (20 mm, dens 15 mm), on thin x2, thick x2 and thin suite18:\n- no dens: +0.00, -0.09, -0.28\n- flush, back 10 degrees: -0.79, -0.38, -0.90\n- recessed 2 mm, back 10 degrees: +3.90, +4.88, +3.69 (old +5.12, +6.25, +5.00)\n\nWindow share above the body's top: 35 to 50 per cent of the window and 22 to 41 per cent of the edges. Freedom in the dens cases: 0.21 to 0.84 degrees. No posterior_wall or posterior_wall_free flag in any dens case; the only wall flag in these runs was posterior_wall_free, on the small body with no dens at 2.5 mm.\n\nC2 row errors on thin x2, no dens then flush, recessed and recessed-leaning dens:\n- endplate_lower_incl: -3.37, then -5.86, -11.47, -7.27\n- spinous_angle: +0.54, then +3.31, +8.79, +4.48"
