        "detail": "posterior_wall's docstring quotes figures from \"the 98 phantom vertebrae of the scratch cache of 30 October\": 4.14/5.66, 0.35/0.79, 97 of 98 parting, the 0.16/0.10 rank correlation, 1.64/3.03 on the alterations, 2.56/3.97 ragged, 11.7 for the lip. phantom.py line 42 says \"as the wall-slope study of 30 October drew it\". The pedicle docstring and the WALL_TOL_DEG comment point to \"the phantom vertebrae that function names\", and the README's new bullet (line 69) says the alterations and bounds \"are in its docstring\". The scripts that make these numbers (build_cache.py, score.py, judge/robust.py, judge/proposed.py) exist only under the session scratchpad, and no file in the repository names them or the configurations: PHANTOMS, LEANS, the seven GRIDS, SPACINGS 0.8x0.8x1 and 0.86x0.86x2.5, and the 12 alterations. The usual home for such a record is research_notes/morphometry/handoff.md, which by precedent names scratch probes as \"not committed\". It was left unchanged, and still says \"Tracing that reading is the next step, before any real run reads either\" (lines 47-51) and lists \"1. Trace body.posterior_wall_slope ...\" first under What is left (line 75). A next account resuming from the handoff would redo this work.",
        "fix": "Add a dated pass to handoff.md, or a section of research_notes/morphometry/README.md, that records the cache's configurations, the alteration list with their parameters and the scratch scripts by name (marked not committed), mark What is left item 1 done, and point the docstring's \"scratch cache\" and phantom.py's \"wall-slope study\" at that record. Or commit the scorer.",
        "lens": "words",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds. It is about traceability, not about the code's output, and every part of it can be checked against the tree.\n\n1. The handoff still lists this work as next. The change leaves research_notes/morphometry/handoff.md untouched (the diff stat lists eight files and it is not one of them). Line 51 still says \"Tracing that reading is the next step, before any real run reads either\", and line 75 still begins \"What is left, in order\" with \"1. Trace body.posterior_wall_slope on the grids aligned with the frame\". The implementer called the handoff \"a dated record\" and left it alone, but the repository's own history says otherwise. Each earlier engine pass added a pass of its own to handoff.md in the same commit: 0e847cd added 82 lines and 9e565c4 added 86. a9f21fd went further and edited the 01:45 section in place. That earlier pass also recorded its scratch probe by name as \"not committed\" (line 18, probe_interval), which is the precedent the reviewer cites.\n\n2. The numbers' source is not in the repository. Outside archive/, grep finds none of build_cache, robust.py or proposed.py, nor suite18, the grid names or the alteration names. The one score.py it finds is schwingcheck's, which is unrelated. \"scratch cache\" appears only in body.py line 325, and \"wall-slope study\" only in phantom.py line 42. The docstring says what the cache is made of only in loose prose: four phantoms, the wall upright, leaned 5 degrees either way or 4 forward, seven grids \"from aligned with the frame to 25 degrees off\", 1 and 2.5 mm slices, and the alterations by name. The exact settings sit only in the scratchpad: the grid tilt axes, angles and turns in build_cache.GRIDS, the shapes phantom's parameters, and 9 of the 12 alteration parameter sets in robust.ALTERATIONS. Only the ragged edge and the lip carry parameters in the docstring. So nobody can reproduce 0.35/0.79, 4.14/5.66, \"97 of 98\", 0.16/0.10 or 1.64/3.03 from the repository. The suite checks only the 0.5-degree bound on two phantoms.\n\nOne part is slightly overstated. \"No file names the configurations\" is too strong: the docstring names them loosely, and the two spacings appear in the suite. They are not tied to the cache there, though, and this does not change the finding.\n\nMeasured as a side check: does the committed phantom draw what the scratch cache drew? Yes. phantom.py's new lean parameter matches build_cache.sheared voxel for voxel. On the x2 and suite18 grids, at lean +5 and -5, on both thin and thick spacing, 0 voxels differed and the affines were equal. So the phantom comment (\"as the wall-slope study of 30 October drew it\") is true, and committing a record of the configuration list (or the scorer) would be enough to rebuild the cache. That supports the reviewer's fix; it does not refute the defect.",
            "measured": "The committed P.lumbar(lean=L) against build_cache.sheared(L), on grids x2 and suite18, L = +5 and -5, thin (0.8x0.8x1) and thick (0.86x0.86x2.5): 8 drawings, 0 voxels differing in each (23945415 / 8464918 voxels on x2, 32804200 / 11466126 on suite18), affines equal. Script: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_9_0/same_drawing.py; log: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_9_0/same_drawing.log. The grep results above were run over /home/user/bordercall, excluding archive/ and .git."
