      {
        "title": "BasivertebralNotch does not test the robust fit its docstring says it tests",
        "severity": "medium",
        "file": "morphometry/tests/test_morphometry.py",
        "line": 392,
        "detail": "The docstring says the notch floor's edges enter the fit and 'the trimmed fit must leave them out'. But the notch runs 3 mm either side of mid-height, symmetric about the middle of the window, so its edges move the fit's intercept and not its slope.\n\nI replaced trimmed_start and biweight with one plain least-squares fit (mutants.py nolts, log mut_nolts.log). Both notch tests and both LeanedWall tests still pass. The notch wall reads -0.257 degrees off (robust fit: -0.205); the label no longer parts, and 121 of 217 edges lie more than a step from the fit.\n\nThe same notch drawn 4 mm above or below mid-height (review_tests/notch_up.py, logs notch_up.log and notch_up2.log) gives:\n- robust fit: -0.035 and -0.358\n- plain fit: +8.899 and -7.021\nSo the off-centre placement, the notch_z parameter the implementer dropped, is what would make this test bite.\n\nNeither placement pins trimmed_start itself. A plain fit used as the biweight's start reads exactly what the trimmed start reads at 0 and at +4 and -4 mm. The biweight is pinned only by LeanedWall: with the biweight taken out (mutants.py nobiweight) its wall reads 3.00 off and the test fails.",
        "fix": "Restore notch_z in phantom.Vert and draw the class's notch 4 mm below mid-height; keep WALL_TOL_DEG. That placement also reaches the unparted path (next finding). Rewrite the docstring to say what the case pins. If the trimmed start is meant to matter, add a case where a plain start followed by the biweight fails, and show that it fails.",
        "lens": "tests",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "The claim holds. I measured it on phantoms myself, with scripts I wrote, not the reviewer's.\n\n1. The notch test passes with no robust fit at all. I replaced the robust fit with one plain least-squares fit: body.trimmed_start became np.linalg.lstsq and body.biweight became the identity. I then ran BasivertebralNotch as the suite has it (verify_11_0/plain_suite.py). Both tests pass: 2 run, OK, 19.1 s.\n\n2. The notch is symmetric about the middle of the window. With the notch at mid-height as the suite draws it, the window runs from -8.05 to 8.14 mm (centre +0.04). Of 217 edges, 38 lie on the notch floor. Their z from the window's centre runs from -2.87 to +2.62, mean -0.04. So the floor's edges shift the plain fit's intercept but barely its slope:\n   - wall error: -0.205 degrees with the fit as it stands, -0.257 with the plain fit (bound 0.5)\n   - endplate_incl and endplate_lower_incl: +0.13 and +0.22 with the plain fit (bound 1.5), so they pass too\n   - the plain fit does not part the label, and leaves 121 of 217 edges more than a step from the surface; the fit as it stands parts it and drops 44, matching the reviewer\n\n3. Off-centre, the plain fit fails and the robust fit holds:\n\n   | Notch centre | Plain fit: wall | Plain fit: inclinations | Fit as it stands: wall |\n   |---|---|---|---|\n   | 4 mm up | +8.899 | -9.00 / -8.88 | -0.035 |\n   | 4 mm down | -7.021 | +6.91 / +6.99 | -0.358 |\n\n   At 4 mm down the fit as it stands does not part the label, so it reaches the unparted path, as the reviewer said. It is within the 0.5 bound by a margin of only 0.14 degrees.\n\n4. No placement pins trimmed_start itself:\n   - A plain start followed by the biweight reads exactly what the fit as it stands reads, at 0, +4 and -4 mm.\n   - The trimmed start with no biweight also passes at all three placements: -0.205, -0.143 and -0.102 degrees.\n   - Moving the notch off-centre therefore pins only that some robust step is there, not the trimmed fit the docstring names.\n   - The reviewer's separate log (mut_nobiweight.log) shows LeanedWall failing at -3.00 degrees with the biweight taken out. I did not rerun that one.\n\nSo the docstring's claim, that the notch floor's edges enter the fit and \"the trimmed fit must leave them out\", is not something this test checks with the notch at mid-height.\n\nLogs: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_11_0/fits.log and /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/verify_11_0/plain_suite.log.",
            "measured": "Notch at mid-height, plain least-squares fit (no trimmed start, no biweight): both BasivertebralNotch tests pass; wall error -0.257 degrees (as it stands -0.205), inclination errors +0.13 / +0.22. The 38 notch-floor edges' mean z is -0.04 mm from the window's centre (-8.05 to 8.14).\nNotch 4 mm up: plain fit wall +8.899, inclinations -9.00 / -8.88 (both fail); as it stands -0.035.\nNotch 4 mm down: plain fit wall -7.021, inclinations +6.91 / +6.99 (both fail); as it stands -0.358, label not parted.\nPlain start followed by the biweight: identical to the fit as it stands at all three placements.\nTrimmed start with no biweight: -0.205, -0.143 and -0.102, all pass."
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "refuted": [],
    "unverified": [
      {
        "title": "The paths real masks take are never reached: unparted labels, the freedom flag and real refusals",
        "severity": "medium",
        "file": "morphometry/tests/test_morphometry.py",
        "line": 376,
        "detail": "Every reading parts the label: I logged all 29 wall readings in a full run (mutants.py none, mut_none.json, summarised by review_tests/paths.py), so clean_digitisation never runs. The largest freedom is 2.32 degrees, below the 3-degree flag (WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG) and the 9-degree refusal (WALL_FREEDOM_DEG). No reading is refused.\n\nThe refusal test gets its NaN only by patching read_wall. So no test reaches the refusals inside wall_window and read_wall, the freedom refusal, or their wording. No test asserts the posterior_wall_free flag, or the flag:posterior_wall and flag:spinous rows that measure_volume writes.\n\nI ran the full suite with four changes at once: clean_digitisation made to raise, both freedom bounds at infinity, and kept_wall ignoring its keys (mutants.py nodigit+noflag+norefuse+stalecache, mut_gates.log). All 31 tests pass.\n\nposterior_wall's own docstring says labels with flipped boundary voxels or a ragged edge almost never part. On a segmenter's masks, then, the clean_digitisation freedom is what decides both the refusal and the flag, and the suite never runs it. An example of an unparted case: the notch 4 mm below mid-height leaves the label unparted, with the wall 0.358 off and the freedom 0.363 degrees (notch_down.log).",
        "fix": "Add three cases:\n(a) An unparted case, such as the notch 4 mm below mid-height: assert info['parted'] is false, the wall error within WALL_TOL_DEG, and a finite freedom.\n(b) A case whose freedom passes 3 degrees: assert flag:posterior_wall_free in measure_volume's rows. The implementer's notes say 3 of the 49 cached 2.5 mm phantoms qualify; I have not measured which.\n(c) A real refusal through measure_volume, with no mock: assert flag:posterior_wall, and NaN for both inclinations, both pedicle sagittal angles and the spinous angle.",
        "lens": "tests"
      },
      {
        "title": "The free flag says the angles 'may be off by up to half' the freedom, but clamped readings sit at an end of the range and err by more than half",
        "severity": "low",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 603,
        "detail": "The reading is not the middle of the parted range. Where the fit lies outside the range, the clamp puts the reading at one end, so the bound the range gives is its full width, not half. And where the label does not part there is no bound at all. On the clean cache, 22 of 98 readings err by more than half their freedom, 15 of them clamped to an end. Example: straight_lean+5_x2_thick has range [+1.57, +3.02] (freedom 1.46), reading +1.57 at the lower end, truth +2.36, error -0.79. The worst ratio is 0.75 of the freedom (straight_lean+5_suite18_thick). The comment on WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG (3 degrees, 'half of it past the suite's 1.5') rests on the same halving. Log: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/review_geometry/range_check.log.",
        "fix": "Say 'may be off by up to that', and only where the label parts. If the intent is to flag what may pass the suite's 1.5 degrees, set WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG to 1.5 and fix the constant's comment.",
        "lens": "geometry"
      },
      {
        "title": "The flag says angles may be off by half the freedom; the clamp allows the whole width",
        "severity": "low",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 603,
        "detail": "Where the label parts, b is clamped into [b_lo, b_hi], so it can stand anywhere in that range, ends included. The error is bounded by the whole width, not half. The flag text written to every CSV (flag:posterior_wall_free, 'the angles taken against the wall may be off by up to half that') overstates the precision. So does WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG's reasoning ('half of it past the suite's 1.5 degrees'), which lets readings with freedom just under 3 go unflagged, though they may be off by nearly 3. On the clean cache, 22 of the 98 vertebrae err beyond half their freedom, up to 0.75 of it: straight_lean+5_suite18_thick reads -0.58 with freedom 0.77, and straight_lean+5_x2_thick -0.79 with 1.46. A flagged case beyond the text's bound: a thoracic T8 phantom on 2.5 mm slices, grid 2 degrees off, wall upright, reads -2.07 with freedom 4.07, where the flag promises at most 2.03. Logs: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/review_robustness/half_freedom.log, thor_thick.log.",
        "fix": "Say 'may be off by up to that', not half. If the flag is meant to mark readings that may pass the suite's 1.5 degrees, set WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG to 1.5. Fix the wall_flags docstring and the constant's comment to match.",
        "lens": "robustness"
      },
      {
        "title": "The canal line's 18.7 degrees is the notch, not the staircase the words set it beside",
        "severity": "low",
        "file": "morphometry/column.py",
        "line": 128,
        "detail": "column.wall_corner says the canal line \"traces the lattice's staircase as the old reading of the wall did and leans on its own, 18.7 degrees on a phantom with a basivertebral notch 4 mm deep\". The README's third standing limit (line 75) says the same: \"reads each slab's wall on the same staircase ... and leans on its own (18.7 degrees on the phantom with the notch)\". The 18.7 comes from the notch's floor being read as the canal's front: on the plain suite phantom the line leans -0.02 degrees (my probe, review_words/notch_probe.py: slope -0.3392 with the notch, -0.0004 without). anatomy.wall_slope's own docstring keeps the two causes apart correctly. The staircase lean that was actually measured is never quoted. On the 98 cached vertebrae (review_words/canal_line.py), the canal line against the true wall in the measured frame errs up to 3.62 degrees on 1 mm slices (x2 grid, median 3.01) and 7.72 on 2.5 mm (x2, median 4.50; aligned grid 5.74). That is worse on thick slices than the old wall reading's 5.66, which bears on its being \"next to fix\", since it cuts the body and sets Panjabi's depths.",
        "fix": "Give both causes with their numbers, for example \"traces the lattice's staircase (up to 3.6 degrees off on 1 mm slices and 7.7 on 2.5 mm, on the grid tilted 2 degrees), and through a basivertebral notch 4 mm deep it leans 18.7\", in column.wall_corner and the README limit.",
        "lens": "words"
      },
      {
        "title": "The docstring leaves out the stronger ragged edge, the largest error but the lip's",
        "severity": "low",
        "file": "morphometry/body.py",
        "line": 369,
        "detail": "\"Two alterations go further\" quotes only the ragged edge at 0.3 mm over 3 mm (2.56 and 3.97, and 0.74 and 1.67 less the label's lean). The judge's alteration set, which the docstring says it reports, also includes ragged_0.5_4, an edge rough by 0.5 mm over 4 mm. It reads up to 5.43 on 1 mm and 6.97 on 2.5 mm, and 1.05 and 2.57 less the lean (judge/prop_table.txt; the same in the implementer's robust rows by robust_summary.py). Apart from the lip, that is the largest error over all twelve alterations. The README sends readers to this docstring for \"what still moves it (a segmenter's ragged edge ...)\".",
        "fix": "Add the 0.5 mm over 4 mm case: \"and one rough by 0.5 mm over 4 mm up to 5.43 and 6.97, 1.05 and 2.57 less that lean\".",
        "lens": "words"
      },
      {
        "title": "The README's conclusion on thick slices no longer matches its refreshed disc rows",
        "severity": "low",
        "file": "research_notes/morphometry/README.md",
        "line": 60,
        "detail": "The change refreshed every row of the 2.5 mm bench table, and the disc rows moved from -0.82/-0.58/-0.59 mm to -0.37/-0.29/-0.30 mm (impl/bench_after.log). The sentence after the table, \"So heights and discs gain or lose about a quarter of a slice on thick slices\", was left as it was. On 2.5 mm slices the discs now lose 0.29 to 0.37 mm, an eighth of a slice. The heights gain 0.50 to 0.63 mm, a fifth to a quarter.",
        "fix": "\"So heights gain about a fifth to a quarter of a slice on thick slices and discs lose about an eighth, with errors up to about one slice.\"",
        "lens": "words"
      },
      {
        "title": "The README's suite line has the new count of 31 but not the phantoms that make it up",
        "severity": "low",
        "file": "research_notes/morphometry/README.md",
        "line": 43,
        "detail": "The rewritten sentence says \"The 31 tests require ...\" and lists the phantoms behind them. The suite's 31 tests (verified: impl/suite.log, Ran 31, OK) include Overhang (a superior articular process over the isthmus's outer columns) and LateralMass (a cervical lateral mass, a short free pedicle). The list names neither, and PitchedDown is at most \"angled pedicles\". The count was brought up to date while the list stayed as it was, so the line no longer describes the suite it counts.",
        "fix": "Add the overhanging process and the cervical lateral mass (and the pitched-down pedicle) to the list in the same sentence.",
        "lens": "words"
      }
    ],
    "fix": {
      "fixed": [
        "Finding 1 (HIGH, a notch over a quarter of the edges, about 29 degrees off with no flag). body.fit_refused now refuses the wall when more than WALL_DROPPED_SHARE = 0.4 of the band's edges lie more than a step off the fit. The 8x6x5 notch is now refused, with shares of 0.66 on 1 mm slices and 0.69 on 2.5 mm on the suite grid, and 0.50 on 2.5 mm on the grid tilted 2 degrees. Before the fix it read -29.15, +29.49 and -29.74. The largest dropped share of any accepted reading over the cache, clean or altered, is 0.26 on 1 mm slices and 0.36 on 2.5 mm, so the rule refuses nothing there. New test DeepNotch runs the 8x6x5 notch on 2.5 mm slices through measure_volume. It checks flag:posterior_wall and flag:spinous, NaN endplate_incl, endplate_lower_incl and spinous_angle, and a finite wedge. With the rule turned off, the test fails.",
        "Finding 2 (HIGH, C2's wall read on the dens). At C2, wall_window now ends the window at the top of the canal's full rings. I added a dens to the phantom (Vert.dens_r, dens_h, dens_y, dens_lean, and dens_inside) and a cervical vertebra about the size of a C5 (phantom.cervical). Test case: a dens 1.5 mm in front of the wall, upright, on a grid tilted 2 degrees. Without the bound it read 8.08 degrees off on 1 mm slices and 7.77 on 2.5 mm; with it, 0.02 and 0.05. Across the dens shapes and grids I probed, the unbounded reading was off by up to 8.43 degrees. With the bound, the worst was -0.94 on the 18-degree grid, where the same C2 with no dens reads -0.74. New test DensAtC2 fails without the bound (8.08 off).",
        "Finding 3 (MEDIUM, HiGHS status 4 read as an unbounded range). wall_slope_range now first solves a widest-margin program, as pedicle.parting does. It also treats any status other than 0 or 3 as no parting, instead of an unbounded end. I measured it: on the 294 one-speck labels on 1 mm slices, 2 got status 4, with margins of 7.4e-8 and 8.5e-8 mm. The old code refused the one with status 4 on both programs, reporting it 'free over 180 degrees'; it now reads +0.016. New test SpeckOnTheMargin fails with the old range code, and the other wall tests still pass under the old code.",
        "Finding 4 (MEDIUM, the clamp was all or nothing). wall_freedom now sets aside the 1, 2 or at most WALL_ACROSS_MAX = 3 edges the fit leaves furthest across, ranked by fit_crossing, before falling back to the clean-digitisation freedom. Test: one speck behind each of six spread edges in turn, on every cached vertebra. 3 of 294 labels no longer part on 1 mm slices and 1 of 294 on 2.5 mm, against 169 of 288 and 155 of 294 without the set-aside. The largest error with a speck is 1.30 degrees. New test LeanedWall.test_specks_behind_the_wall: 2 specks keep the reading unchanged, and 8 leave the label unparted, still read within TOL_DEG. It fails with WALL_ACROSS_MAX set to 0.",
        "Finding 7 (MEDIUM, freedom bounds nothing on unparted labels). The docstring now says the freedom is a bound only where the label parts. For unparted labels it gives how often the truth fell inside the range: 21 of 49 (1 mm) and 31 of 49 (2.5 mm) with flipped voxels; 7 of 49 and 16 of 47 with the 0.3 mm ragged edge. The free-wall flag text now comes from a new helper, free_meaning: on an unparted label it says the width bounds nothing. A unit test in FreeWall covers this.",
        "Findings 8, B and C (the word 'half'). The posterior_wall_free flag now says the angles 'may be off by up to that' (the whole freedom) where the label parts. The WALL_FREE_FLAG_DEG comment now gives the threshold as twice the suite's 1.5 degrees, since a reading may lie anywhere in its range.",
        "Finding 9 (band and window words). The WALL_BAND_SHARE and WALL_BAND_MAX_MM comments and the docstrings now call the band's value a half-width: it reaches either side of the midline, half the canal's width in all. The window is described as the middle WALL_HEIGHT_SHARE of the height that the body's points in the band span, with the C2 bound. The WALL_GATE_SHARE comment now gives the canal line's error on a clean wall and the notch's lean.",
        "Finding 10 (numbers resting on an unrecorded scratch cache, and handoff.md listing this work as next). I added a section at the top of handoff.md, '14:00 UTC'. It records the cache's configuration: 4 phantoms with their leans, 7 grids and 2 spacings, 98 vertebrae in all. It also records the 12 alterations with their parameters, the speck study, the probes, and the scratch scripts by name, marked as not committed. Item 1 of the 01:45 list is marked done. body.posterior_wall's docstring, phantom.py's lean comment and the README now point at that section.",
        "Finding 11 (MEDIUM, no test pins the clamp). New test LeanedWall.test_the_clamp checks that the label parts, that the reading lies inside the parted range, and that it is within 0.25 degrees. The clamped reading is -0.097 off; the fit alone is -0.406 off, which still passes the 0.5-degree WALL_TOL_DEG. With the clamp removed, the new test fails.",
        "Finding 12 (MEDIUM, BasivertebralNotch did not test the robust fit). The phantom now has Vert.notch_z, and the test draws the notch 4 mm below mid-height. There a plain least-squares fit reads the wall 7.02 degrees off and is refused for 51 per cent of edges off; at mid-height it read -0.26, so the old test could not tell the fits apart. The robust fit reads -0.36 and the clamped reading -0.15. The canal line leans 6.10 there. New test test_the_fit_leaves_the_notch_out checks the fit before the clamp. With a plain fit, 2 of the class's tests fail and 1 errors.",
        "Finding A (the paths real masks take were not reached). DeepNotch is a real refusal through measure_volume. FreeWall runs the free flag through measure_volume (lean 5, grid tilted 5 degrees about x, 2.5 mm slices; freedom 7.87 on the same build), checks the flag row, and checks the end-plate angles are still read. The unparted path is reached by the 8-speck case and the unparted flag text.",
        "Finding D (the canal line's 18.7 degrees is the notch, not the staircase). column.wall_corner, anatomy.wall_slope and the README's third limit now give both causes. The staircase puts the line up to 3.74 degrees off the true wall on 1 mm slices and 7.72 on 2.5 mm over the cache. The notch's floor leans it 18.74 degrees through the 5x6x4 notch at mid-height.",
        "Finding E. posterior_wall's docstring now includes the ragged edge of 0.5 mm over 4 mm: up to 5.43 and 6.97 degrees, and 1.05 and 2.57 once the edge's own lean is taken off. The other figures in the docstring are refreshed to the final reader: clamped 0.35/0.79 (medians 0.07/0.19), fit alone 1.24/1.18, truth inside the range on 97 of 98 clean readings, rank correlation 0.18/0.12, 3 of 49 thick clean readings flagged, alterations up to 1.64/3.03. pedicle.py's module paragraph now gives the clamped and the fit-alone figures.",
        "Finding F. The README's thick-slice sentence now matches the bench rows: heights gain 0.50 to 0.63 mm (a fifth to a quarter of a 2.5 mm slice), discs lose 0.29 to 0.37 mm (an eighth to a seventh), largest error 2.40 mm. A fresh bench --slice 2.5 run reproduced the table exactly.",
        "Finding G. The README's suite line now names PitchedDown, Overhang, LateralMass, LeanedWall, SpeckOnTheMargin, BasivertebralNotch, DeepNotch, FreeWall and DensAtC2, and says 39 tests in 428 s."
      ],
      "not_fixed": [
        {
          "title": "Finding 6 (MEDIUM): on cervical bodies the 6 mm band reaches the pedicle roots",
          "why": "Left as it was and documented. On the cervical C5 phantom (canal 24 mm, body 17 mm) the band is the full 6 mm either side, 12 of the body's 17 mm. Over 0 and 2 degrees about x, 5 degrees, and 18 degrees, leans 0 and plus or minus 5, and pedicles 2 mm up or down, the wall still reads within 1.47 degrees on 1 mm slices and 1.32 on 2.5 mm. I tried a cap at a quarter of the body's width either side: 0.92 and 1.72. It helps thin slices and hurts thick ones, so I reverted it. Both pairs of numbers are in the docstring, the constant's comment and the handoff. The C5 sizes are the reviewer's, not a published table, and no real cervical mask was read."
        },
        {
          "title": "Finding 5 (MEDIUM): the gate is centred on a canal line that a notch leans",
          "why": "Partly addressed. The gate is still centred on the canal line, which a notch leans (18.74 degrees through the 5x6x4 notch at mid-height). I tried a second gate pass centred on the first fit. It hid the dropped share: the 8x6x5 notch on 2.5 mm slices, on the grid tilted 2 degrees, then showed a share of 0.40 and read -29.7 degrees with no refusal. So I reverted it. The dropped-share refusal (finding 1) now catches what a leaned gate lets through, and the WALL_GATE_SHARE comment says so. Fixing the cause means reading the canal line on the lattice, which is next in the handoff."
        },
        {
          "title": "Residual of finding 1: the share rule refuses some sound walls",
          "why": "A 5x10x4 mm notch is refused on 3 of the 4 grid and thickness combinations I probed. Shares: 0.51 on 1 mm slices on the suite grid, where the reading had been 0.19 off; 0.52 on 1 mm slices on the 2-degree grid; 0.46 on 2.5 mm slices on the 2-degree grid. It is read (0.36 off) on 2.5 mm slices on the suite grid. No threshold separates these cases: the 8x6x5 notch at a share of 0.50 read -29.74, so admitting 0.52 would let it through. The docstring and handoff record this false refusal."
        },
        {
          "title": "Related to finding 7: no flag where the label does not part",
          "why": "Deliberately not added. Of the 294 cache readings with flipped voxels or a ragged edge, all but 5 are unparted, so such a flag would fire almost every time and say little. Instead, the free-wall flag text says the freedom bounds nothing on an unparted label, and the docstring gives the truth-inside counts."
        }
      ],
      "suite": "I committed nothing. The changes are in the working tree of /home/user/bordercall, branch claude/project-thread-e89pjg. Final full suite (run with PYTHONPATH=. OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python -m unittest -v morphometry.tests.test_morphometry): 39 tests, all pass, in 428 s on four CPUs. Log: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/fix/suite2.log. During that run I re-wrapped the test module's docstring; no code changed. An earlier full run without SpeckOnTheMargin passed 38 of 38 in 444 s (suite.log in the same folder). Bite check (/tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/fix/bite.py), each fix undone in turn: a plain least-squares fit makes BasivertebralNotch fail (2 failures, 1 error); removing the clamp fails 2 of LeanedWall's 4 tests; removing the C2 bound fails DensAtC2 (8.08 degrees off); setting the set-aside to 0 fails 1 of 4 in LeanedWall; removing the share rule errors DeepNotch (no flag); the old wall_slope_range fails SpeckOnTheMargin ('free over 180 degrees') while the other 11 wall tests pass. The status 4 depends on the solver, here scipy 1.18.1 with numpy 2.5.4. python -m morphometry bench --slice 2.5 reproduces the README table exactly (bench_final.log). All 9 changed files are ASCII with LF line endings and compile.",
      "score": "score.py, run as cache current current on the reader as it now stands (log: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/wall/fix/score_final.log). Errors in degrees; \"old\" is the hindmost-voxel reading. Thin (0.8x0.8x1 mm): 49 of 49 read; largest 0.35, median 0.07, mean 0.09 (old: 4.14, median 0.40). Largest by grid: aligned 0.18, suite18 0.13, t25 0.04, x10 0.13, x2 0.25, x5 0.35, y12 0.20. Sagittal angle: largest 2.03 over 81 readings. Thick (0.86x0.86x2.5 mm): 49 of 49 read; largest 0.79, median 0.19, mean 0.21 (old: 5.66, median 0.39). Largest by grid: aligned 0.20, suite18 0.58, t25 0.41, x10 0.22, x2 0.79, x5 0.68, y12 0.31. No sagittal angle is read on thick slices. Every clean label parts, one of them with an edge set aside. The truth falls inside the parted range on 97 of 98. The fit alone erred up to 1.24 (median 0.10) on thin slices and 1.18 (median 0.19) on thick. 3 of the 49 thick readings are flagged as free; no thin reading is.",
      "morphoview": "python -m unittest morphoview.tests.test_lines: 10 tests, all pass, in 25.4 s."
